scheduler::schedule_from_remote, which another thread calls to wake a fiber
of this scheduler's thread, puts the fiber on the remote ready queue under
the queue's spinlock, lets the spinlock go, and then notifies the
scheduling algorithm, through algo_, a member of the scheduler. Between the
two, the scheduler's own thread may take the fiber from the queue, run it
to its end, finish, and destroy the scheduler at thread exit, so the notify
uses what is freed: round_robin::notify signals a condition variable it
has let its mutex go before, which ThreadSanitizer reports destroyed under
the signal. Notified before the spinlock goes, the scheduler's thread,
which takes the spinlock to see the fiber, cannot run it until the notify
is done. Boost's develop and master still notify after.

diff --git a/src/scheduler.cpp b/src/scheduler.cpp
index 69ae61c..0ec5dae 100644
--- a/src/scheduler.cpp
+++ b/src/scheduler.cpp
@@ -208,8 +208,9 @@ scheduler::schedule_from_remote( context * ctx) noexcept {
     BOOST_ASSERT( nullptr != dispatcher_ctx_.get() );
     // push new context to remote ready-queue
     ctx->remote_ready_link( remote_ready_queue_);
-    lk.unlock();
-    // notify scheduler
+    // notify scheduler before letting the spinlock go: this scheduler's
+    // thread takes it to see ctx, so cannot run ctx, end, and destroy this
+    // scheduler and its algorithm while the notify is still using them
     algo_->notify();
 }
 #endif
